    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_001",
      "title": "Sex Linkage and Sexual Dimorphism",
      "nav_label": "Sex Linkage and Sexual Dimorphism",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_001 · Sex Linkage and Sexual Dimorphism",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_001",
      "text_preview": "In sexual species, males and females often differ with respect to the mean and variance of traits. In addition, the relative contribution of the different components of variance may differ between the sexes. There are two reasons for this. First, in the case of sex-linked genes, females have two possible genes at each…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_002",
      "title": "SEX-LINKED LOCI AND DOSAGE COMPENSATION",
      "nav_label": "SEX-LINKED LOCI AND DOSAGE COMPENSATION",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_002 · SEX-LINKED LOCI AND DOSAGE COMPENSATION",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_002",
      "text_preview": "In the case of a specific sex-linked locus, the genotypic value of a male with allele i can be written $$ G {M i}^{\\prime}=\\mu {M}+\\alpha {M i}^{\\prime} \\tag{24.1a} $$ where $ \\mu M $ is the mean phenotype of males, and $ \\alpha' {Mi} $ is the average effect of the ith allele measured as a deviation from $ \\mu M $, wi…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_003",
      "title": "SEX-MODIFIED EXPRESSION OF AN AUTOSOMAL LOCUS",
      "nav_label": "SEX-MODIFIED EXPRESSION OF AN AUTOSOMAL LOCUS",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_003 · SEX-MODIFIED EXPRESSION OF AN AUTOSOMAL LOCUS",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_003",
      "text_preview": "Just as the expressed effect of a sex-linked gene may vary between the sexes, so may the expression of any autosomal gene. For a single autosomal locus, we denote the genotypic values of females and males respectively as $$ G {F i j}=\\mu {F}+\\alpha {F i}+\\alpha {F j}+\\delta {F i j} \\tag{24.2a} $$ $$ G {M i j}=\\mu {M}+…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_004",
      "title": "SEX-MODIFIED EXPRESSION OF AN AUTOSOMAL LOCUS / Gametic Imprinting",
      "nav_label": "Gametic Imprinting",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_004 · SEX-MODIFIED EXPRESSION OF AN AUTOSOMAL LOCUS / Gametic Imprinting",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_004",
      "text_preview": "Before proceeding, we mention a rather different type of sex modification of gene action. Over the past few years, several studies, mostly in transgenic mice, have demonstrated the phenomenon of gametic imprinting, whereby certain genes carry a memory of their gametic origin and are expressed differently in progeny de…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_005",
      "title": "EXTENSION TO MULTIPLE LOCI AND THE COVARIANCE BETWEEN RELATIVES",
      "nav_label": "EXTENSION TO MULTIPLE LOCI AND THE COVARIANCE BETWEEN RELATIVES",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_005 · EXTENSION TO MULTIPLE LOCI AND THE COVARIANCE BETWEEN RELATIVES",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_005",
      "text_preview": "As first shown by Bohidar (1964), it is a relatively straightforward procedure to extend the general model of Kempthorne (1954) to describe polygenic situations in which sex linkage and sex modification of gene expression are involved. The only modification of the description of the phenotypic resemblance between rela…"
    },
    {
      "id": "Genetics_chapter24_006",
      "title": "VARIATION FOR SEXUAL DIMORPHISM",
      "nav_label": "VARIATION FOR SEXUAL DIMORPHISM",
      "raw_title": "Genetics_chapter24_006 · VARIATION FOR SEXUAL DIMORPHISM",
      "level": 2,
      "depth": 1,
      "anchor": "genetics_chapter24_006",
      "text_preview": "In the previous sections, we recognized the fact that many characters are sexually dimorphic. However, we did not consider whether the dimorphism itself is evolutionarily labile, as opposed to being a fixed difference that inevitably results from physiological and/or hormonal differences between males and females. Sex…"
    }
  ],
